y.\n    for start, end in sorted_intervals[1:]:\n        last_start, last_end = result[-1]\n        if start <= last_end:\n            result[-1] = (min(last_start, start), max(last_end, end))  # Update the last interval directly.\n        else:\n            result.append((start, end))\n    return result\n```\nThis version avoids creating a new list for `current` and instead updates the last element in the `result` list directly when intervals merge, which reduces both time complexity (by avoiding unnecessary copying) and space complexity (by not maintaining an additional data structure).",
